\p{So what kind of proofs do we want, and what kind do we want to reject?  As an example, consider the proposition "for every natural number, there is a larger number which is not a square".  Intuitively, this proposition shows that there are infinitely many non-squares: no matter where we are on the number line, we can always find a non-square farther along.  We might encode this in formal propositional logic as follows:

